Cycle 3A: 14-runner synthesis - scalar moat plus endpoint pressure
  observation=the best cell-blocker is one scalar puncture away from equality, while the best interval-cover disproof is a seven-ladder with many exposed endpoints.
  candidate_theorem=any speed set close enough to the Dirichlet/scalar spine to cover the unit layer inherits either the 56-cell scalar moat or the seven-ladder endpoint leak.
  dead_end=this is not a proof yet; it needs a bridge between residue-vector cell blocking and endpoint-cover leakage.
  forced_new_15_idea=test whether the bridge should use divisor coordinates 6/14 for n=15.

Cycle 3B: 15-runner synthesis - divisor-coordinate warning
  observation=n=15 punctures prefer coordinates 6 and 14 with jumps 5 or 10; these are exactly the 3*5 divisor jumps, not random coordinates.
  candidate_theorem=for composite n, minimal scalar punctures live on coordinates whose gcd with n exposes a proper quotient, and their jump is a complementary divisor layer.
  dead_end=still local around scalar ramps; far-from-scalar blockers need a search certificate.
  forced_disproof_idea=record a reusable next search: endpoint cycles first, scalar distance second.

Cycle 3C: disproof route - next reproducible search specification
  1. choose a finite leak layer L, starting with {a/14, 9/98, 29/182, 15/182};
  2. find a directed protection cycle on L using the integer protection inequality;
  3. minimize scalar distance of realizing speeds to avoid the 56/120 scalar moats;
  4. only then run exact interval audits.

Final synthesis
  A. 14-runner route: local scalar repair is dead; the 56-cell moat is robust.
  B. 15-runner route: the transferred moat thickens to 120 and singles out divisor coordinates.
  C. disproof route: quotient ladders give tiny measure gaps but fail endpoint protection.
  D. new loop invariant: scalar distance and endpoint-protection closure must be optimized together.
